[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [1mssh-agent  [22mis a program to hold private keys used for public key authenti‐
       cation.  Through use of environment variables the agent can be located and
       automatically used for authentication when logging in  to  other  machines
       using [4mssh[24m(1).

       The options are as follows:

       [1m-a [4m[22mbind_address[0m
               Bind  the  agent  to the Unix-domain socket [4mbind_address[24m.  The de‐
               fault  is  to  create  a  socket  at  a   random   path   matching
               [4m$HOME/.ssh/agent/s.*[24m.

       [1m-c      [22mGenerate C-shell commands on standard output.  This is the default
               if SHELL looks like it's a csh style of shell.

       [1m-D      [22mForeground  mode.   When  this option is specified, [1mssh-agent [22mwill
               not fork.

       [1m-d      [22mDebug mode.  When this option is  specified,  [1mssh-agent  [22mwill  not
               fork and will write debug information to standard error.

       [1m-E [4m[22mfingerprint_hash[0m
               Specifies  the  hash  algorithm  used  when displaying key finger‐
               prints.  Valid options are: “md5” and “sha256”.   The  default  is
               “sha256”.

       [1m-k      [22mKill  the  current  agent  (given by the SSH_AGENT_PID environment
               variable).

       [1m-O [4m[22moption[0m
               Specify an option when starting [1mssh-agent[22m.  The supported  options
               are: [1mallow-remote-pkcs11[22m, [1mno-restrict-websafe [22mand [1mwebsafe-allow[22m.

               The  [1mallow-remote-pkcs11  [22moption  allows  clients  of  a forwarded
               [1mssh-agent [22mto load PKCS#11 or FIDO provider libraries.  By  default
               only  local  clients  may  perform this operation.  Note that sig‐
               nalling that an [1mssh-agent [22mclient is remote is performed by [4mssh[24m(1),
               and use of other tools to forward access to the agent  socket  may
               circumvent this restriction.

               The  [1mno-restrict-websafe [22moption instructs [1mssh-agent [22mto permit sig‐
               natures using FIDO keys that might be web authentication requests.
               By default, [1mssh-agent [22mrefuses signature  requests  for  FIDO  keys
               where  the  key  application string does not start with “ssh:” and
               when the data to be signed does not appear to be  an  [4mssh[24m(1)  user
               authentication request or an [4mssh-keygen[24m(1) signature.  The default
               behaviour  prevents  forwarded  access to a FIDO key from also im‐
               plicitly forwarding the ability to authenticate to websites.

               Alternately the [1mwebsafe-allow [22moption allows specifying a  pattern-
               list of key application strings to replace the default application
               allow-list,                      for                      example:
               “websafe-allow=ssh:*,example.org,*.example.com”

               See PATTERNS in [4mssh_config[24m(5) for a  description  of  pattern-list
               syntax.

       [1m-P [4m[22mallowed_providers[0m
               Specify  a  pattern-list  of acceptable paths for PKCS#11 provider
               and FIDO authenticator middleware shared  libraries  that  may  be
               used  with  the [1m-S [22mor [1m-s [22moptions to [4mssh-add[24m(1).  Libraries that do
               not match the pattern list will be refused.  The default  list  is
               “usr/lib*/*,/usr/local/lib*/*”.

               See  PATTERNS  in  [4mssh_config[24m(5) for a description of pattern-list
               syntax.

       [1m-s      [22mGenerate Bourne shell commands on standard output.   This  is  the
               default if SHELL does not look like it's a csh style of shell.

       [1m-T      [22mBind  the  agent  socket  in a randomised subdirectory of the form
               [4m$TMPDIR/ssh-XXXXXXXXXX/agent.<ppid>[24m, instead of the default behav‐
               iour of using a randomised name matching [4m$HOME/.ssh/agent/s.*[24m.

       [1m-t [4m[22mlife[0m
               Set a default value for the maximum lifetime of  identities  added
               to  the  agent.   The lifetime may be specified in seconds or in a
               time format specified in [4msshd_config[24m(5).  A lifetime specified for
               an identity with [4mssh-add[24m(1) overrides this  value.   Without  this
               option the default maximum lifetime is forever.

       [1m-U      [22mInstructs  [1mssh-agent  [22mnot  to  clean  up stale agent sockets under
               [4m$HOME/.ssh/agent/[24m.

       [1m-u      [22mInstructs [1mssh-agent [22mto only clean up  stale  agent  sockets  under
               [4m$HOME/.ssh/agent/[24m  and  then  exit immediately.  If this option is
               given twice, [1mssh-agent [22mwill delete stale agent sockets  regardless
               of the host name that created them.

       [4mcommand[24m [[4marg[24m [4m...[24m]
               If  a  command (and optional arguments) is given, this is executed
               as a subprocess of the agent.  The agent exits automatically  when
               the command given on the command line terminates.

       There  are  three  main  ways to get an agent set up.  The first is at the
       start of an X session, where all other windows or programs are started  as
       children of the [1mssh-agent [22mprogram.  The agent starts a command under which
       its  environment  variables  are  exported, for example [1mssh-agent xterm &[22m.
       When the command terminates, so does the agent.

       The second method is used for a login session.  When [1mssh-agent [22mis started,
       it prints the shell commands required to set  its  environment  variables,
       which  in  turn  can  be  evaluated in the calling shell, for example [1meval[0m
       [1m`ssh-agent -s`[22m.

       In both of these cases, [4mssh[24m(1) looks at these  environment  variables  and
       uses them to establish a connection to the agent.

       The third way to run [1mssh-agent [22mis via socket activation from a supervising
       process,  such  as systemd.  In this mode, the supervising process creates
       the listening socket and is responsible for starting [1mssh-agent [22mas  needed,
       and  also  for  communicating the location of the socket listener to other
       programs in the user's session.  Socket activation is used when  [1mssh-agent[0m
       is  started with either of the [1m-d [22mor [1m-D [22mflags, no socket listening address
       specified by the [1m-a [22mflag, and both the LISTEN_FDS and LISTEN_PID  environ‐
       ment variables correctly supplied by the supervising process.

       The  agent initially does not have any private keys.  Keys are added using
       [4mssh-add[24m(1) or by [4mssh[24m(1) when [1mAddKeysToAgent [22mis set in [4mssh_config[24m(5).  Mul‐
       tiple identities may be stored in [1mssh-agent [22mconcurrently and  [4mssh[24m(1)  will
       automatically use them if present.  [4mssh-add[24m(1) is also used to remove keys
       from [1mssh-agent [22mand to query the keys that are held in one.

       Connections  to [1mssh-agent [22mmay be forwarded from further remote hosts using
       the [1m-A [22moption to [4mssh[24m(1) (but see the caveats documented therein), avoiding
       the need for authentication data to be stored on other machines.   Authen‐
       tication  passphrases and private keys never go over the network: the con‐
       nection to the agent is forwarded over SSH remote connections and the  re‐
       sult is returned to the requester, allowing the user access to their iden‐
       tities anywhere in the network in a secure fashion.

       [1mssh-agent [22mwill delete all keys it has loaded upon receiving SIGUSR1.

[1mENVIRONMENT[0m
       SSH_AGENT_PID  When  [1mssh-agent  [22mstarts,  it stores the name of the agent's
                      process ID (PID) in this variable.

       SSH_AUTH_SOCK  When [1mssh-agent [22mstarts, it creates a Unix-domain socket  and
                      stores  its  pathname  in  this variable.  It is accessible
                      only to the current user, but is easily abused by  root  or
                      another instance of the same user.

       In  Debian,  [1mssh-agent [22mis installed with the set-group-id bit set, to pre‐
       vent [4mptrace[24m(2) attacks retrieving private  key  material.   This  has  the
       side-effect  of  causing the run-time linker to remove certain environment
       variables which might have security implications for set-id programs,  in‐
       cluding  LD_PRELOAD,  LD_LIBRARY_PATH, and TMPDIR.  If you need to set any
       of these environment variables, you will need to do so in the program exe‐
       cuted by ssh-agent.

[1mFILES[0m
       [4m$HOME/.ssh/agent/s.*[0m
               Unix-domain sockets used to contain the connection to the  authen‐
